The only use this has is to keep salaries low.

&#x200B;

What they really want is for you to low-ball yourself so that you can both agree you are worth less than you actually are. Failing that, if they know you make $50k a year, your ask for $100k a year from them is very negotiable. They "know" they can get you for $55-$60. Then the cycle continues - They low ball everyone until someone accepts. And then during the next round of hiring, their "salary range" stays where it was. So they can low ball the next applicant. 

&#x200B;

You owe yourself honesty from yourself - Don't negotiate against yourself. Decide what salary would actually put you in that job and allow you to stay there with at least a level of happiness above your current position. Then demand $10k above that. Let them bargain you down, but don't go lower than your happiness number. If they won't pay you what it would take to increase your happiness, you don't really want that job.

your listed monthly expense is $3,000 and you take home $2,800. that's a huge red flag that you're spending more than you make. are you just charging up around 1,500 in cc expenses each month, or do you have a large cc debt that you're paying down? if you're racking up 1500 in charges, then you're spending way more than you should be. if you have a large amount of cc debt, then i would pause your savings and focus on paying that down first.  cc's generally carry really bad interest rates. 

&#x200B;

as for your savings, once you've eliminated your debt, i would put more into the roth. you can save up for a house down payment later. you can never get back the missed opportunity of contributing to your roth IRA.

